      "speaker": "SPEAKER_00",
      "text": "The other day my colleague asked in one of our internal chats, guys I have an Israeli client and he's asking me is it safe for me as an Israeli citizen to invest in the UAE because what do you think about the situation of 2017 when UAE had a conflict with Qatar and at that point they even closed the airspace between each other, all the economic the economies were actually on stop between the two countries. So his question was very simple, did UAE take away the freehold ownership rights from the Qatari citizens and that question guide got me really excited. I went to Chad GPT and I started researching what happened there now initially I didn't know because I'm a citizen of another country next to a step government. never came across this question before. But what I realize is that there was the Arabian Spring in 2011 -12 after which there were some differences in the political vision between the Qatar and the UAE and in 2017 it evolved into a conflict where the Qatar citizens were given briefly like three weeks to get out of the country. Although this was true and the conflict lasted till 2021, which after then it was fully recovered and citizens of Qatar were allowed back, during this time UAE never took away the rightful ownership of those Qatar citizens. This gave me a little hint about Dubai. It's not just how we we as people who live here as brokers see the potential future of it. It's the history of Dubai that tells us that look at all these questions on a long run. Each time the government is making decisions towards stability of the economy, of the citizens and keeping safety of the citizens. So they know even if there is a conflict here and now today, in the future it will be gone. They will need to live with it. So they're taking their decisions very cautiously, thoughtful.",
